  "image_name": "42254_2025_917_Fig1_HTML.webp",
  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates a pump–probe optical setup combined with nonlinear time gating to isolate signals from a sample. Two ultrafast pulses, labeled A and B, arrive at different times, with their separation defined as the difference between probe and pump arrival times. Both pulses travel through the sample, where they appear to generate a combined response along the same beam path.\n\nAfter the sample, the light is directed toward a nonlinear crystal or mixing region, where it overlaps with a separate gate pulse arriving at a controllable time. The timing relationships are emphasized with labeled delays for the probe and pump relative to the gate, showing that the detected signal depends on when the gate pulse samples the transmitted light. This effectively gives a time-resolved measurement window.\n\nAt the nonlinear interaction point, the incoming fields from A and B and the gate pulse produce a new signal labeled \\(E_{NL}\\), with a relation indicating that it is formed from the combined field minus the individual contributions. The final mixed signal is sent to a detector, likely an EOS detector. Overall, the image conveys how pulse timing and gated nonlinear detection are used to separate and measure ultrafast sample dynamics.",
